<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Portable Windows UPDATE-FEED verification core (Phase 5 · signed distribution).

    This is the Windows analog of the macOS Sparkle update path, reduced to the
    PLATFORM-INDEPENDENT security kernel: parse the direct-download update feed, verify a
    detached Ed25519 signature over a canonical release descriptor with a PINNED public key
    that is INDEPENDENT of the Authenticode code-signing certificate, and decide —
    fail-closed — whether an authenticated, newer release is available.

    Parity + threat model
    ─────────────────────
      • macOS: Sparkle signs the DMG bytes with EdDSA (Curve25519) and pins SUPublicEDKey in
        Info.plist, independent of the Developer-ID codesign cert. A compromised signing cert
        alone cannot ship a malicious update — the appcast enclosure must ALSO carry a valid
        EdDSA signature under the pinned key.
      • Windows: Authenticode (Azure Artifact Signing) authenticates the
        installer to SmartScreen and the OS loader. This library adds the SECOND, INDEPENDENT
        control: a pinned Ed25519 signature over a canonical descriptor that BINDS the
        artifact's sha256 (content), url, size, version, channel, minimum OS build, and the
        critical flag. So — exactly as on macOS — a compromised Authenticode cert alone cannot
        push an update: the feed entry must ALSO verify under the pinned update key, and the
        downloaded bytes must hash to the signed sha256. Either check failing is fail-closed
        (never "available").

    Ed25519 comes from BouncyCastle.Cryptography (pure-managed, no native deps) — the SAME
    approved dependency the Computer-Use input path (windows/pal/input) and the CloudVault
    crypto (windows/cloudsync) already ship, and the SAME primitive as macOS CryptoKit
    Curve25519.Signing over the same canonical bytes. It restores + runs + is unit-tested on
    the macOS authoring host today (windows/tests/dist) via `dotnet test`.

    net8.0 (no Windows TFM, zero P/Invoke, zero OS calls) so the WinUI app + the
    OpenBurnBar.Dist.UpdateFeed.Tool signer reference it, and the net10.0 test project
    consumes it too.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <RootNamespace>OpenBurnBar.Dist.UpdateFeed</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>OpenBurnBar.Dist.UpdateFeed</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Pure-managed Ed25519 (matches macOS CryptoKit Curve25519.Signing over the same
         canonical bytes). No native deps: restores + runs on macOS CI and Windows. -->
    <PackageReference Include="BouncyCastle.Cryptography" Version="2.4.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OpenBurnBar.Dist.UpdateFeed.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
